# Flutter Style Guide

- Prefer double quotes (`"`) instead of single quotes (`'`) for all strings in Dart/Flutter code.
- Prefer `final` and `var` over explicit type definition.
- Do not use the `labelText` property on text fields. Use only `hintText` for placeholder text.
- When editing a file with code generation, always run `dart run build_runner build -d` afterwards.
- Always use absolute imports, NEVER USE RELATIVE IMPORTS!

- Widgetbook utilities:
  - In stories and use-cases, wrap content with `FakeApp` (widgetbook/lib/widgetbook_utils.dart). Supply provider overrides via its `overrides` parameter. Do not build ad-hoc `MaterialApp`/`ProviderScope` stacks in stories.
  - `FakeApp` already applies `AppRequiredWidgets`, `Responsive`, app-wide shortcuts (`TypewriterPanel.typewriterShortcuts`), and scroll behavior. Avoid duplicating these wrappers.

- Test utilities:
  - In widget tests, use `testApp(...)` and `WidgetTester.pumpTestApp(...)` (test/test_utils.dart) instead of hand-rolled scaffolds.
  - Use `WidgetTester.captureScreenshot(...)` for PNG captures during tests; keep outputs under "test_screenshots".
  - Reuse provider mocks from the testkit package: import `package:typewriter_testkit/typewriter_testkit.dart` and use the provided overrides helpers (e.g., `appearanceProviderOverrides(...)`, `authProviderOverrides(...)`, `booksProviderOverrides(...)`, `manualsProviderOverrides(...)`, `modulesProviderOverrides(...)`, `organizationsProviderOverrides(...)`).
  - Call `setupMocks()` (test/test_utils.dart) in your test main or setUpAll to register mocktail fallback values when needed.

- Import exceptions (narrowly scoped):
  - The rule to always use package imports still applies everywhere.
  - Widgetbook and Testkit exception: importing `package:riverpod/src/framework.dart` is permitted strictly to reference `Override` in the Widgetbook workspace and within the `typewriter_testkit` package (mocks). In those locations, include the ignore annotations `// ignore: depend_on_referenced_packages, implementation_imports` on that import line. Do not use implementation imports in app/lib code, and do not re-export them.
  - Tests may use `dart:io` APIs when needed for local file outputs (e.g., screenshots). Keep such usage confined to the test code paths.
